window.UAI_MICRO = {"lessons": [{"id": "w1d1", "week": 1, "day": 1, "t": "What counts as AI?", "l": "This curriculum groups AI into four paradigms. Predictive AI estimates what will happen (will this claim be fraudulent?). Prescriptive AI recommends what to do (which claims to inspect first). Generative AI creates new content such as text or images. Multimodal AI combines several data types, such as images plus notes. Most real systems chain them: perceive, reason, act.", "r": [9], "s": "Read the abstract and the first two sections.", "y": "List three AI tools you used this month and label each one predictive, prescriptive, generative, or multimodal.", "q": "Which paradigm answers 'what should we do?' rather than 'what will happen?'", "a": "Prescriptive AI. It turns predictions into recommended decisions."}, {"id": "w1d2", "week": 1, "day": 2, "t": "Learning means generalizing", "l": "Domingos' central point: a model is only useful if it works on data it has never seen. Learning is representation plus evaluation plus optimization. Memorizing the training data is easy; generalizing is the hard part. That is why we always hold back test data.", "r": [9], "s": "Read the sections 'It's generalization that counts' and 'Data alone is not enough'.", "y": "Write one sentence explaining why a model that scores 100% on its training data might still be useless.", "q": "Why do we keep a separate test set?", "a": "To measure performance on unseen data, which is the only performance that matters in use."}, {"id": "w1d3", "week": 1, "day": 3, "t": "Your first Python program", "l": "Python runs instructions top to bottom. A variable is a name for a value: rate = 0.05. Types matter: 3 is an int, 3.0 a float, '3' a string. You can run Python in your browser with Google Colab or any free notebook, with nothing to install.", "r": [12], "s": "Read Think Python, Chapter 1.", "y": "In a notebook, compute the monthly payment idea: principal = 1000, rate = 0.05, print(principal * rate). Then change the values.", "q": "What is the type of '42' in Python?", "a": "A string (str), because it is in quotes. 42 without quotes is an int."}, {"id": "w1d4", "week": 1, "day": 4, "t": "Functions and decisions", "l": "A function packages steps you reuse: def area(w, h): return w * h. An if/elif/else block chooses a path based on a condition. Loops (for, while) repeat steps. Together these cover most of the logic you will write in this course.", "r": [13], "s": "Watch 6.100L Lecture 2 (branching and iteration), first half.", "y": "Write a function grade(score) that returns 'pass' if score >= 70, otherwise 'retry'. Test it on three values.", "q": "What does a for loop do that an if statement does not?", "a": "It repeats a block of code for each item, while an if runs a block at most once based on a condition."}, {"id": "w1d5", "week": 1, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: paradigms and Python basics", "l": "Retrieval beats rereading. Without looking back, recall the four paradigms, the meaning of generalization, and the three building blocks of Python logic (variables, conditionals, loops). Then check your notes.", "r": [12], "s": "Skim the Think Python Chapter 2 and 3 summaries to fill any gaps.", "y": "Draft your 1-page 'AI paradigms' map: one box per paradigm, one real example each, and arrows showing how they chain.", "q": "Name the four AI paradigms used in this curriculum.", "a": "Predictive, prescriptive, generative, and multimodal."}, {"id": "w2d1", "week": 2, "day": 1, "t": "Dictionaries: data by name", "l": "A dictionary maps keys to values: policy = {'id': 17, 'premium': 450}. Lookups by key are fast and readable. Real-world records (a row in a spreadsheet, a JSON response from an API) map naturally onto dictionaries.", "r": [12], "s": "Read Think Python, the chapter on dictionaries (Chapter 10).", "y": "Make a dict of three products and their prices, then loop over it and print the item with the highest price.", "q": "How do you get the premium from policy = {'premium': 450}?", "a": "policy['premium'], or policy.get('premium') to avoid an error if the key is missing."}, {"id": "w2d2", "week": 2, "day": 2, "t": "Classes and objects", "l": "A class is a blueprint that bundles data and behavior. class Account: holds a balance and a deposit() method. Each account you create is an object. You will mostly use classes that libraries give you, such as a Pandas DataFrame or a scikit-learn model.", "r": [13], "s": "Watch the 6.100L lecture introducing object-oriented programming, first 15 minutes.", "y": "Write a tiny class Counter with an add() method and a value attribute. Create two counters and show they are independent.", "q": "What is the difference between a class and an object?", "a": "A class is the blueprint; an object is one specific instance made from it."}, {"id": "w2d3", "week": 2, "day": 3, "t": "NumPy: fast arrays", "l": "NumPy stores numbers in arrays and applies math to the whole array at once (vectorization). prices * 1.1 raises every price by 10% without a loop. Almost every AI library is built on top of arrays like these.", "r": [14], "s": "Read Python Data Science Handbook, 'The Basics of NumPy Arrays'.", "y": "Create an array of 10 numbers, then compute its mean, max, and the values above the mean in three lines.", "q": "Why is vectorized NumPy code preferred over Python loops?", "a": "It is shorter and much faster, because the work runs in optimized compiled code."}, {"id": "w2d4", "week": 2, "day": 4, "t": "Pandas: tables you can query", "l": "A DataFrame is a table with named columns. You load a CSV with pd.read_csv, filter rows with a condition, group with groupby, and plot with .plot(). This is the everyday tool of data work.", "r": [14], "s": "Read Python Data Science Handbook, 'Introducing Pandas Objects' and 'Aggregation and Grouping'.", "y": "Load any public CSV (for example a city open-data file), then show the first five rows and one groupby summary.", "q": "Which Pandas method summarizes data by category?", "a": "groupby, followed by an aggregation such as mean() or count()."}, {"id": "w2d5", "week": 2, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: trees and forests preview", "l": "A decision tree asks a sequence of yes/no questions to reach a prediction. A random forest trains many trees on random samples of the data and averages their votes. That makes it more accurate and stable than one tree. You will build these properly in Week 4.", "r": [15], "s": "Read the Random Forests abstract and introduction.", "y": "Finish your EDA notebook: one cleaned dataset, three charts, and three written observations.", "q": "Why does a random forest usually beat a single decision tree?", "a": "Averaging many trees trained on different random samples reduces variance, so predictions are more stable."}, {"id": "w3d1", "week": 3, "day": 1, "t": "The data lifecycle", "l": "Data moves through stages: collect, clean, explore, model, communicate, and maintain. Most project time goes into cleaning. Mistakes made early (a biased sample, a mislabeled column) flow into every later stage.", "r": [11], "s": "Watch the first 6.0002 lecture on data science, first 15 minutes.", "y": "Pick one report you use at work and write down which lifecycle stage is weakest.", "q": "Which lifecycle stage usually takes the most time?", "a": "Cleaning and preparing the data."}, {"id": "w3d2", "week": 3, "day": 2, "t": "Tidy data", "l": "Wickham's tidy-data rules: each variable is a column, each observation is a row, and each type of observational unit is a table. Tidy data is easy to filter, group, and plot. Messy spreadsheets often put values in column headers, such as one column per month.", "r": [16], "s": "Read Tidy Data, Sections 1 to 3.", "y": "Find a 'wide' table (for example months as columns) and reshape it to long format with pd.melt.", "q": "State the three tidy-data rules.", "a": "Each variable is a column, each observation is a row, and each observational unit is a table."}, {"id": "w3d3", "week": 3, "day": 3, "t": "Simpson's paradox", "l": "A trend can reverse when you split data into groups. In the 1973 Berkeley admissions data, men appeared to be admitted at a higher overall rate, yet most departments admitted women at similar or higher rates. Women had applied more often to competitive departments. Always ask what variable might be hiding.", "r": [17], "s": "Read the Bickel, Hammel and O'Connell paper's opening pages and the departmental table.", "y": "Build a small table in Pandas where the overall rate favors group A but each subgroup favors group B.", "q": "What caused the reversal in the Berkeley data?", "a": "Women applied disproportionately to departments with low admission rates; department was a confounding variable."}, {"id": "w3d4", "week": 3, "day": 4, "t": "Charts that tell the truth", "l": "Good charts maximize the data-ink ratio: remove gridlines, 3D effects, and decoration that carry no information. Start bar-chart axes at zero. Label directly instead of using a legend when you can. Choose the chart for the comparison you want the reader to make.", "r": [14], "s": "Read Python Data Science Handbook, 'Simple Line Plots' and 'Customizing Plot Legends'.", "y": "Take one chart from a recent slide deck and list two changes that would raise its data-ink ratio.", "q": "Why should bar charts start at zero?", "a": "Bar length encodes the value; a truncated axis exaggerates differences."}, {"id": "w3d5", "week": 3, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: reproducible analysis", "l": "A reproducible analysis runs end to end from raw data to final chart with one click. Keep raw data untouched, put every cleaning step in code, and note your assumptions. Anyone (including you in six months) should get the same result.", "r": [18], "s": "Browse The Analytics Edge course page and pick one case to read later.", "y": "Clean up your notebook so it runs from top to bottom with Restart and Run All.", "q": "What is the single test of a reproducible notebook?", "a": "It produces the same results when run from a fresh start, top to bottom."}, {"id": "w4d1", "week": 4, "day": 1, "t": "Linear regression", "l": "Linear regression fits a straight-line relationship: predicted value = intercept + weights times inputs. Each weight says how much the prediction changes when that input rises by one unit, holding the others fixed. It is simple, fast, and easy to explain.", "r": [19], "s": "Read ISLP Chapter 3, Section 3.1.", "y": "Fit a regression with scikit-learn on any numeric dataset and print the coefficients. Interpret one in plain English.", "q": "What does a regression coefficient of 2.5 on 'years of experience' mean?", "a": "Each extra year is associated with a 2.5-unit higher prediction, holding other inputs constant."}, {"id": "w4d2", "week": 4, "day": 2, "t": "Logistic regression and risk", "l": "Logistic regression predicts a probability between 0 and 1, which suits yes/no outcomes such as 'heart disease within ten years'. The Framingham Heart Study used this kind of model to turn risk factors such as age, blood pressure, and smoking into a risk score.", "r": [18], "s": "Watch The Analytics Edge Framingham Heart Study lecture segment.", "y": "Write down three inputs you would use to predict customer churn, and whether each should raise or lower the risk.", "q": "Why use logistic rather than linear regression for yes/no outcomes?", "a": "Its output is a probability bounded between 0 and 1."}, {"id": "w4d3", "week": 4, "day": 3, "t": "Decision trees (CART)", "l": "CART splits the data on the question that best separates outcomes, then repeats on each branch. Trees are easy to read and handle nonlinear patterns, but deep trees overfit. Limit depth or prune them.", "r": [19], "s": "Read ISLP Chapter 8, Section 8.1.", "y": "Fit a DecisionTreeClassifier with max_depth=3 and print the tree with export_text.", "q": "What is the main risk of a very deep decision tree?", "a": "Overfitting: it memorizes noise in the training data and generalizes poorly."}, {"id": "w4d4", "week": 4, "day": 4, "t": "Confusion matrix and ROC", "l": "A confusion matrix counts true positives, false positives, true negatives, and false negatives. Precision asks how many flagged cases were real; recall asks how many real cases were caught. The ROC curve shows the trade-off across every threshold, and AUC summarizes it in one number.", "r": [20], "s": "Read An Introduction to ROC Analysis, Sections 1 to 3.", "y": "For a fraud model, decide whether false positives or false negatives cost more, and say which way you would move the threshold.", "q": "What does an AUC of 0.5 mean?", "a": "The model ranks no better than random guessing."}, {"id": "w4d5", "week": 4, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: clustering and the model card", "l": "Unsupervised learning finds structure without labels. k-means assigns points to k groups by distance to group centers; hierarchical clustering builds a tree of merges. Use them for segmentation, then describe each segment in business terms.", "r": [24], "s": "Read the scikit-learn user guide section on k-means.", "y": "Phase 1 checkpoint: write a one-page model card for one classifier, covering purpose, data, metrics, limitations, and intended use.", "q": "What is the key input you must choose for k-means?", "a": "k, the number of clusters."}, {"id": "w5d1", "week": 5, "day": 1, "t": "The neuron", "l": "An artificial neuron multiplies each input by a weight, adds them up with a bias, and passes the total through an activation function. Stack many neurons in layers and the network can learn complex patterns, such as recognizing handwritten digits.", "r": [26], "s": "Watch 3Blue1Brown Neural Networks, Chapter 1.", "y": "Sketch a network with 3 inputs, one hidden layer of 4 neurons, and 1 output. Count the weights.", "q": "What does the activation function add to a network?", "a": "Nonlinearity, so stacked layers can learn patterns a straight line cannot."}, {"id": "w5d2", "week": 5, "day": 2, "t": "Gradient descent", "l": "Training means finding weights that make the loss (error) small. Gradient descent computes which direction reduces the loss and takes a small step that way, over and over. The learning rate controls the step size: too big overshoots, too small crawls.", "r": [26], "s": "Watch 3Blue1Brown Neural Networks, Chapter 2.", "y": "Explain gradient descent to a colleague using a hiker finding the bottom of a valley in fog.", "q": "What happens if the learning rate is too high?", "a": "Training overshoots the minimum and the loss bounces around or diverges."}, {"id": "w5d3", "week": 5, "day": 3, "t": "Backpropagation", "l": "Backpropagation is the chain rule applied efficiently. It passes the error backward through the network to work out how much each weight contributed. Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams showed in 1986 that this lets hidden layers learn useful internal representations.", "r": [26, 28], "s": "Watch 3Blue1Brown Neural Networks, Chapter 3, and read the 1986 paper's abstract.", "y": "Write the three steps of one training iteration: forward pass, compute loss, backward pass and update.", "q": "What mathematical rule does backpropagation rely on?", "a": "The chain rule of calculus."}, {"id": "w5d4", "week": 5, "day": 4, "t": "Overfitting and generalization", "l": "Underfitting means the model is too simple to capture the pattern. Overfitting means it memorizes the training data and fails on new data. Watch the gap between training and validation loss. Remedies include more data, regularization, and early stopping.", "r": [25], "s": "Read Nielsen, Chapter 3, the section on overfitting and regularization.", "y": "Draw the typical training and validation loss curves and mark where you would stop training.", "q": "What is the sign of overfitting on a loss chart?", "a": "Training loss keeps falling while validation loss starts rising."}, {"id": "w5d5", "week": 5, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: embeddings", "l": "An embedding represents an item, such as a word, as a list of numbers so that similar items end up close together. Word2vec showed that such vectors capture relationships, such as king minus man plus woman landing near queen. Embeddings power search, recommendations, and LLMs.", "r": [29], "s": "Read the Mikolov et al. abstract and introduction.", "y": "Write an annotated sketch of backpropagation for a single neuron: inputs, weight, output, loss, and the gradient flowing back.", "q": "What property makes embeddings useful?", "a": "Similar items have nearby vectors, so distance measures similarity."}, {"id": "w6d1", "week": 6, "day": 1, "t": "Tensors and PyTorch", "l": "PyTorch stores data in tensors, which are multi-dimensional arrays that can run on a GPU and track gradients automatically (autograd). You define a model, a loss, and an optimizer, then loop: predict, compute loss, call backward, and step.", "r": [34], "s": "Do the PyTorch 'Learn the Basics' tutorial, Tensors page.", "y": "Create a tensor with requires_grad=True, compute y = x squared, call y.backward(), and print x.grad.", "q": "What does autograd do?", "a": "It records operations on tensors and computes gradients automatically for backpropagation."}, {"id": "w6d2", "week": 6, "day": 2, "t": "Loss functions", "l": "The loss function defines what 'wrong' means. Use mean squared error for numeric targets and cross-entropy for classification. Choosing the loss is a design decision: it encodes what you want the model to care about.", "r": [32], "s": "Read Dive into Deep Learning, the softmax regression section on the loss function.", "y": "For a model that predicts claim cost, decide whether large errors should be punished more, and pick MSE or MAE.", "q": "Which loss is standard for classification?", "a": "Cross-entropy."}, {"id": "w6d3", "week": 6, "day": 3, "t": "Optimizers: SGD and Adam", "l": "Stochastic gradient descent updates weights using small random batches, which is fast and adds helpful noise. Adam adapts the step size for each weight using running averages of past gradients, and it is a strong default.", "r": [35], "s": "Read the Adam paper abstract and Algorithm 1.", "y": "Train the same small model twice, once with SGD and once with Adam, and compare loss after five epochs.", "q": "Why use mini-batches instead of the whole dataset each step?", "a": "They are faster per step, fit in memory, and the noise can help find better solutions."}, {"id": "w6d4", "week": 6, "day": 4, "t": "Regularization: dropout and batch norm", "l": "Dropout randomly switches off neurons during training, so the network cannot rely on any single one. Batch normalization rescales layer inputs, which stabilizes and speeds up training. Both are standard tools against overfitting and unstable training.", "r": [36, 37], "s": "Read the Dropout paper abstract and Figure 1.", "y": "Add nn.Dropout(0.5) to a small network and note how the gap between training and validation accuracy changes.", "q": "Is dropout active during prediction?", "a": "No. It is used only in training; at inference all neurons are used."}, {"id": "w6d5", "week": 6, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: tuning hyperparameters", "l": "Hyperparameters are settings you choose rather than learn: learning rate, batch size, number of layers, dropout rate. Change one at a time, track results, and keep a validation set separate from the final test set.", "r": [33], "s": "Watch fast.ai Lesson 1, the first 20 minutes.", "y": "Write a short training-curve comparison: two learning rates, one chart, and three sentences of interpretation.", "q": "Which hyperparameter is usually the most important to tune first?", "a": "The learning rate."}, {"id": "w7d1", "week": 7, "day": 1, "t": "Convolution", "l": "A convolution slides a small filter across an image and measures how well each patch matches it. Early filters detect edges; later layers combine them into textures, parts, and objects. The same filter is reused everywhere, which saves parameters.", "r": [39], "s": "Read the CS231n notes on convolutional networks, the convolutional layer section.", "y": "On paper, apply a 3x3 vertical-edge filter to a 5x5 grid of numbers and compute two output values.", "q": "Why do CNNs need far fewer parameters than fully connected networks on images?", "a": "Each filter is shared across all positions in the image."}, {"id": "w7d2", "week": 7, "day": 2, "t": "Pooling and feature hierarchies", "l": "Pooling shrinks feature maps, for example by keeping the maximum in each 2x2 block. This makes the network less sensitive to small shifts and cheaper to run. Stacking convolution and pooling builds a hierarchy from edges to objects.", "r": [40], "s": "Read LeCun et al. 1998, the LeNet-5 architecture figure and description.", "y": "Sketch LeNet-5's layer sequence from input to output.", "q": "What does max pooling keep?", "a": "The largest value in each small region."}, {"id": "w7d3", "week": 7, "day": 3, "t": "The ImageNet breakthrough", "l": "In 2012 AlexNet won the ImageNet challenge by a wide margin using a deep CNN, GPUs, ReLU activations, and dropout. ResNet (2015) added skip connections, which let networks reach over 100 layers without training breaking down.", "r": [41, 42], "s": "Read the ResNet abstract and look at the residual-block figure.", "y": "In two sentences, explain why a skip connection helps a very deep network learn.", "q": "What problem do ResNet skip connections address?", "a": "Very deep networks becoming harder to train; the shortcut lets signals and gradients flow through."}, {"id": "w7d4", "week": 7, "day": 4, "t": "Transfer learning", "l": "Features learned on millions of images transfer to new tasks. Yosinski et al. showed early layers are general and later layers more task-specific. In practice you take a pretrained model, replace the final layer, and train on your small dataset.", "r": [43], "s": "Read the Yosinski et al. abstract and conclusion.", "y": "List one image problem at your organization (for example damage photos) that could use a pretrained model.", "q": "Which layers are most general and transferable?", "a": "The early layers, which detect basic features such as edges and textures."}, {"id": "w7d5", "week": 7, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: fine-tuning MobileNetV2", "l": "MobileNetV2 is a small, efficient CNN built for phones. To fine-tune it, freeze the pretrained layers, train a new classifier head, and optionally unfreeze the top layers with a low learning rate.", "r": [44], "s": "Read the MobileNetV2 abstract.", "y": "Phase 2 checkpoint: outline your transfer-learning notebook, covering dataset, frozen layers, new head, and the metric you will report.", "q": "Why use a low learning rate when unfreezing pretrained layers?", "a": "To adjust the learned features gently instead of destroying them."}, {"id": "w8d1", "week": 8, "day": 1, "t": "Predict versus prescribe", "l": "A prediction tells you what is likely; a prescription tells you what to do about it. Knowing which customers may leave is predictive. Choosing which offer to give each one, within budget, is prescriptive. Value comes from better decisions, not better forecasts alone.", "r": [45], "s": "Read Bertsimas and Kallus, the abstract and introduction.", "y": "Take one dashboard KPI and write the decision it should drive.", "q": "Give a one-line difference between predictive and prescriptive analytics.", "a": "Predictive estimates outcomes; prescriptive recommends actions to improve them."}, {"id": "w8d2", "week": 8, "day": 2, "t": "From predictions to prescriptions", "l": "Bertsimas and Kallus use machine learning (for example nearest neighbors or trees) to weight past similar situations, then choose the decision that would have done best across them. This links the data you have directly to the decision you need.", "r": [45], "s": "Read Section 2 of From Predictive to Prescriptive Analytics.", "y": "Describe in three steps how you would use past orders to set tomorrow's stock level.", "q": "What does the prescriptive approach use past similar cases for?", "a": "To estimate how each candidate decision would perform, then pick the best."}, {"id": "w8d3", "week": 8, "day": 3, "t": "Optimal policy trees", "l": "A policy tree assigns a treatment (for example which drug or which offer) to each group defined by a few splits. Optimal policy trees are interpretable: a manager can read the rule. They are learned from observational data with careful adjustment for who got what.", "r": [46], "s": "Read the Optimal Policy Trees abstract and the first example figure.", "y": "Write a two-split policy tree for assigning a customer-retention offer.", "q": "Why are policy trees attractive to decision-makers?", "a": "The decision rule is short and readable, so it can be checked and explained."}, {"id": "w8d4", "week": 8, "day": 4, "t": "Predict, then optimize", "l": "Many systems predict first and optimize second, for example forecasting demand and then planning staff. Elmachtoub and Grigas argue the prediction model should be trained on the cost of the decisions it leads to, not just prediction accuracy.", "r": [47], "s": "Read the Smart Predict, then Optimize abstract and introduction.", "y": "Name one case where a small forecast error causes a large decision cost.", "q": "What does 'Smart Predict, then Optimize' change about training?", "a": "It trains the predictor on decision cost rather than pure prediction error."}, {"id": "w8d5", "week": 8, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: policy learning", "l": "Learning policies from historical data is hard because past decisions were not random. Athey and Wager show how to learn treatment policies with guarantees, adjusting for who received which treatment.", "r": [48], "s": "Read the Athey and Wager abstract.", "y": "Write a 1-page memo reframing one business decision as a prescriptive problem: decision, data, objective, constraints.", "q": "Why is observational data tricky for learning policies?", "a": "Past treatments were not randomly assigned, so outcomes are confounded."}, {"id": "w9d1", "week": 9, "day": 1, "t": "Anatomy of an optimization model", "l": "Every optimization model has three parts: decision variables (what you choose), an objective (what you maximize or minimize), and constraints (what limits you). Writing these down clearly is most of the work; solvers do the rest.", "r": [49], "s": "Read Hands-On Mathematical Optimization, the first linear-optimization example.", "y": "Write the variables, objective, and constraints for planning a weekly team rota.", "q": "Name the three parts of an optimization model.", "a": "Decision variables, an objective, and constraints."}, {"id": "w9d2", "week": 9, "day": 2, "t": "Linear programming", "l": "In a linear program the objective and constraints are linear. The best solution sits at a corner of the feasible region, which is why the simplex method walks from corner to corner. Linear programs with millions of variables solve quickly.", "r": [50], "s": "Watch the first 15.053 lecture or read its first set of notes.", "y": "Solve a two-product production problem with OR-Tools or by drawing it on graph paper.", "q": "Where does the optimal solution of a linear program lie?", "a": "At a vertex (corner) of the feasible region."}, {"id": "w9d3", "week": 9, "day": 3, "t": "Shadow prices", "l": "A shadow price tells you how much the objective would improve if a constraint were relaxed by one unit. In airline revenue management it estimates the value of one more seat on a flight. It turns a solver's output into a business conversation.", "r": [18], "s": "Watch The Analytics Edge airline revenue-management segment.", "y": "For your rota model, name the constraint whose shadow price you would most want to know.", "q": "What does a shadow price of zero mean?", "a": "The constraint is not binding; relaxing it would not improve the objective."}, {"id": "w9d4", "week": 9, "day": 4, "t": "Network flows", "l": "Shortest path, assignment, and minimum-cost flow are classic network models. Many come with a bonus called total unimodularity: the LP solution is automatically whole numbers, so you get integer answers without integer programming.", "r": [51], "s": "Read the 15.093J network-flow lecture notes introduction.", "y": "Draw a small delivery network and find the shortest path from depot to customer by hand.", "q": "Why is total unimodularity useful?", "a": "The linear relaxation already gives integer solutions, so problems stay fast to solve."}, {"id": "w9d5", "week": 9, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: diet problem and sensitivity", "l": "The diet problem picks the cheapest food basket that meets nutrition requirements; the World Food Programme has used optimization of this kind in its supply planning. Sensitivity analysis asks how much the answer changes when costs or requirements shift.", "r": [52], "s": "Read the OR-Tools linear-solver getting-started guide.", "y": "Build a three-food diet LP in OR-Tools and report one shadow price.", "q": "What question does sensitivity analysis answer?", "a": "How the optimal solution and value change when inputs change."}, {"id": "w10d1", "week": 10, "day": 1, "t": "Integer decisions", "l": "Some decisions are yes or no: open a warehouse or not. Mixed-integer optimization (MIO) allows whole-number variables. It is far more expressive than linear programming but harder to solve, because the solver must search combinations.", "r": [49], "s": "Read Hands-On Mathematical Optimization, the mixed-integer introduction.", "y": "Write one decision at work that is naturally yes or no and would need an integer variable.", "q": "Why are integer programs harder than linear programs?", "a": "The feasible set is not continuous, so the solver must search over combinations."}, {"id": "w10d2", "week": 10, "day": 2, "t": "Facility location", "l": "Facility location chooses which sites to open and how to serve demand from them, trading fixed opening costs against transport costs. The same model applies to vaccine distribution centers, clinics, and branch networks.", "r": [49], "s": "Read the facility-location example in Hands-On Mathematical Optimization.", "y": "Sketch a facility-location model for three possible sites and five customer areas.", "q": "Which two costs does facility location trade off?", "a": "Fixed costs of opening sites and variable costs of serving demand."}, {"id": "w10d3", "week": 10, "day": 3, "t": "Branch and bound", "l": "Branch and bound solves integer problems by splitting the problem (branching) and using LP relaxations to prove that whole branches cannot beat the best solution found (bounding). That pruning is what makes large problems tractable.", "r": [51], "s": "Read the 15.093J notes on branch and bound, first pages.", "y": "Draw a small branching tree for two yes/no variables.", "q": "What does 'bounding' let the solver do?", "a": "Discard branches that provably cannot beat the current best solution."}, {"id": "w10d4", "week": 10, "day": 4, "t": "Multiple objectives", "l": "Real problems have competing goals: cost versus service, speed versus fairness. A Pareto frontier shows the solutions where you cannot improve one goal without hurting another. The decision-maker then picks a point on it.", "r": [53], "s": "Read Boyd and Vandenberghe, the section on multicriterion optimization (Section 4.7).", "y": "Plot a rough cost-versus-service trade-off for a decision you know and mark your current position.", "q": "What is a Pareto-optimal solution?", "a": "One where no objective can be improved without making another worse."}, {"id": "w10d5", "week": 10, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: convexity and SGD", "l": "A convex problem has one valley, so any local minimum is the global one. Neural network training is not convex, yet mini-batch stochastic gradient descent works well in practice. Bottou, Curtis and Nocedal explain why.", "r": [54], "s": "Read the Bottou, Curtis and Nocedal abstract and introduction.", "y": "Draw your Pareto-frontier chart for a facility or staffing example.", "q": "Why is convexity a big deal in optimization?", "a": "Any local minimum is a global minimum, so the solution found is the best possible."}, {"id": "w11d1", "week": 11, "day": 1, "t": "Joining prediction and optimization", "l": "This integration week chains what you built. A predictive model estimates, for example, the expected cost of each claim; an optimizer then assigns limited adjusters to maximize value. The interface between the two is where most projects break.", "r": [47], "s": "Reread the Smart Predict, then Optimize introduction.", "y": "Choose your pipeline problem (claims triage, staffing, or another) and write its one-sentence goal.", "q": "What does the predictive model hand to the optimizer?", "a": "Estimates, such as expected costs or demand, used as inputs to the objective or constraints."}, {"id": "w11d2", "week": 11, "day": 2, "t": "Design the pipeline", "l": "Map the pipeline: data in, model, predictions, optimizer, decisions, and the metric you will judge it by. Decide what happens when predictions are uncertain or missing.", "r": [45], "s": "Skim the Bertsimas and Kallus examples section.", "y": "Draw the pipeline as boxes and arrows, labeling each input and output.", "q": "Why define the evaluation metric before building?", "a": "So you judge the pipeline by decision quality, not just model accuracy."}, {"id": "w11d3", "week": 11, "day": 3, "t": "Build the predictor", "l": "Keep the first model simple and interpretable, such as logistic regression or a shallow tree. A clean baseline lets you see whether the optimizer, not the model, is limiting results.", "r": [19], "s": "Review ISLP Chapter 4 on classification.", "y": "Train the baseline model and save its predictions to a file.", "q": "Why start with a simple baseline model?", "a": "It is fast, explainable, and shows how much more complex models actually add."}, {"id": "w11d4", "week": 11, "day": 4, "t": "Build the optimizer", "l": "Feed predictions into an LP or MIO, add the real constraints (capacity, budget, rules), and solve. Compare the decisions to a simple rule of thumb, such as 'handle the largest claims first'.", "r": [52], "s": "Review the OR-Tools example you built in Week 9.", "y": "Solve the optimization and compare its value against one rule of thumb.", "q": "What should the optimized decision be compared against?", "a": "A realistic baseline, such as the current rule of thumb."}, {"id": "w11d5", "week": 11, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: write it up", "l": "A good write-up states the decision, the data, the model, the optimizer, the improvement over baseline, and the limitations. Executives read the first paragraph, so lead with the result.", "r": [45], "s": "No new reading. Use this session to write.", "y": "Phase 3 checkpoint: finish the one-page pipeline write-up with a results table.", "q": "What belongs in the first paragraph of a technical write-up for executives?", "a": "The decision improved and the size of the improvement."}, {"id": "w12d1", "week": 12, "day": 1, "t": "Tokens", "l": "LLMs read text as tokens, which are chunks of words. Byte-pair encoding builds a vocabulary by repeatedly merging the most frequent character pairs, so common words become one token and rare words split into pieces. Token counts drive cost and context limits.", "r": [61], "s": "Read the subword-units (BPE) paper abstract and introduction.", "y": "Paste a paragraph into any online tokenizer tool and count tokens versus words.", "q": "Why does BPE split rare words into pieces?", "a": "So any word can be represented with a fixed-size vocabulary of frequent subword units."}, {"id": "w12d2", "week": 12, "day": 2, "t": "Attention", "l": "Attention lets each token look at every other token and decide which ones matter for its meaning. In 'the bank by the river', 'bank' attends to 'river'. Each token computes a query, compares it to the keys of other tokens, and mixes their values.", "r": [57], "s": "Read The Illustrated Transformer, the self-attention section.", "y": "Explain query, key, and value using a library: your question, the catalog labels, and the books.", "q": "In attention, what is compared to decide relevance?", "a": "A token's query vector against the other tokens' key vectors."}, {"id": "w12d3", "week": 12, "day": 3, "t": "The transformer block", "l": "A transformer stacks blocks of multi-head attention and a feed-forward network, with residual connections and layer normalization. Multiple heads let the model track different relationships at once. Vaswani et al. dropped recurrence entirely, which made training highly parallel.", "r": [60], "s": "Read Attention Is All You Need, the abstract and Figure 1.", "y": "Hand-draw the transformer block from Figure 1 and label each part.", "q": "What did the transformer remove that earlier sequence models relied on?", "a": "Recurrence. It processes all positions in parallel using attention."}, {"id": "w12d4", "week": 12, "day": 4, "t": "Predicting the next token", "l": "GPT-style models are autoregressive: they predict the next token, append it, and repeat. Pretraining on vast text teaches grammar, facts, and patterns. GPT-2 showed that a large model trained this way can do many tasks without task-specific training.", "r": [63], "s": "Read the GPT-2 paper abstract and introduction.", "y": "Write a sentence one word at a time and note how each word narrows what can come next.", "q": "What is the single training objective of a GPT-style model?", "a": "Predict the next token given the previous tokens."}, {"id": "w12d5", "week": 12, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: build a GPT", "l": "Karpathy's 'Let's build GPT' video codes a small transformer from scratch in about two hours. Watching even the first part connects tokens, attention, and next-token prediction into one working system.", "r": [64], "s": "Watch the first 15 minutes of 'Let's build GPT' in Neural Networks: Zero to Hero.", "y": "Finish your hand-drawn transformer diagram and add one sentence per component.", "q": "List the three ideas that make a GPT work.", "a": "Tokenization, attention in transformer blocks, and autoregressive next-token prediction."}, {"id": "w13d1", "week": 13, "day": 1, "t": "Pretrain, fine-tune, or prompt", "l": "Pretraining builds general ability from massive text. Fine-tuning adapts a model with task-specific examples. Prompting steers the model with instructions and examples at run time with no training. Most business use starts with prompting.", "r": [66], "s": "Read the GPT-3 paper abstract and the figure comparing zero-, one-, and few-shot learning.", "y": "Pick one task at work and decide which approach fits, with one reason.", "q": "Which approach changes the model's weights: prompting or fine-tuning?", "a": "Fine-tuning. Prompting leaves the weights unchanged."}, {"id": "w13d2", "week": 13, "day": 2, "t": "Decoding and temperature", "l": "At each step the model gives probabilities over next tokens. Greedy decoding picks the top one; temperature reshapes the probabilities; top-k and top-p (nucleus) sampling limit choices to the likeliest tokens. Holtzman et al. showed pure greedy decoding tends to produce dull, repetitive text.", "r": [67], "s": "Read the nucleus-sampling paper abstract and Figure 1.", "y": "Run the same prompt at temperature 0 and at a high temperature, and compare the outputs.", "q": "What does lowering temperature do?", "a": "Makes output more deterministic and focused on the most likely tokens."}, {"id": "w13d3", "week": 13, "day": 3, "t": "Few-shot and chain of thought", "l": "Few-shot prompting adds worked examples to the prompt. Chain-of-thought prompting asks the model to reason step by step, which improves multi-step problems. Kojima et al. found that simply adding 'Let's think step by step' helps.", "r": [68, 69], "s": "Read the Chain-of-Thought paper abstract and Figure 1.", "y": "Test one reasoning question three ways: zero-shot, few-shot, and with 'Let's think step by step'.", "q": "What does chain-of-thought prompting ask the model to do?", "a": "Show intermediate reasoning steps before the final answer."}, {"id": "w13d4", "week": 13, "day": 4, "t": "Alignment: RLHF and DPO", "l": "Raw pretrained models predict text, not helpful answers. RLHF trains a reward model from human preference rankings, then optimizes the LLM against it (InstructGPT). DPO reaches a similar goal directly from preference pairs, without a separate reward model.", "r": [72, 73], "s": "Read the InstructGPT abstract and Figure 2.", "y": "Write two answers to the same question and decide which a human rater should prefer, and why.", "q": "What does DPO remove compared with classic RLHF?", "a": "The separately trained reward model and the reinforcement-learning loop."}, {"id": "w13d5", "week": 13, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: scale and experts", "l": "Scaling laws show loss falls predictably as model size, data, and compute grow; Chinchilla showed many models were undertrained on data. Mixture-of-experts models route each token to a few expert sub-networks, adding capacity without proportional compute.", "r": [70, 71], "s": "Read the Chinchilla paper abstract.", "y": "Record your prompting comparison results in a small table: method, accuracy, notes.", "q": "What was Chinchilla's main finding?", "a": "For a fixed compute budget, models should be smaller and trained on more data than was common."}, {"id": "w14d1", "week": 14, "day": 1, "t": "Productivity evidence", "l": "Field and lab studies show sizable productivity gains from generative AI. In a customer-support study, Brynjolfsson, Li and Raymond found productivity rose about 14% on average, with the largest gains for less experienced workers.", "r": [79], "s": "Read the Generative AI at Work abstract and introduction.", "y": "Name one job family at your organization where novices could gain the most.", "q": "Which workers benefited most in the customer-support study?", "a": "Less experienced and lower-skilled workers."}, {"id": "w14d2", "week": 14, "day": 2, "t": "The jagged frontier", "l": "Dell'Acqua et al. ran an experiment with consultants. AI helped greatly on tasks inside its capabilities but hurt performance on a task just outside them. The boundary is jagged and hard to see, so judgment about when to trust AI matters.", "r": [80], "s": "Read the Navigating the Jagged Technological Frontier abstract.", "y": "List two tasks you would trust AI with and one you would not, with reasons.", "q": "What happened on tasks outside AI's frontier?", "a": "Consultants using AI performed worse than those without it."}, {"id": "w14d3", "week": 14, "day": 3, "t": "Centaurs and cyborgs", "l": "The same study described two working styles. Centaurs divide work, handing some tasks to AI and keeping others. Cyborgs weave AI into every step. Both can work; the risk is uncritical copy-paste either way.", "r": [80], "s": "Read the discussion section on centaur and cyborg practices.", "y": "Decide which style you use most and write one habit to improve it.", "q": "Describe the centaur style in one line.", "a": "A clear division of labor: some tasks go to AI, others stay with the human."}, {"id": "w14d4", "week": 14, "day": 4, "t": "How diffusion makes images", "l": "Diffusion models learn to reverse a gradual noising process. Training adds noise to images step by step; the model learns to remove it. To generate, it starts from pure noise and denoises repeatedly. Stable Diffusion does this in a compressed latent space, and CLIP-style text encoders steer it with your prompt.", "r": [90], "s": "Read Lilian Weng's What Are Diffusion Models?, the first section.", "y": "Generate two images from the same prompt with different guidance settings and note the difference.", "q": "Where does generation start in a diffusion model?", "a": "From random noise, which is denoised step by step."}, {"id": "w14d5", "week": 14, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: creativity", "l": "Boden distinguishes three kinds of creativity: combinational (new combinations of familiar ideas), exploratory (exploring within a style), and transformational (changing the rules of the space). Ask which kind a generative tool is really doing.", "r": [83], "s": "Read the Boden paper abstract and introduction.", "y": "Write a one-page brief on GenAI's impact on one job family: tasks augmented, tasks automated, risks.", "q": "Name Boden's three types of creativity.", "a": "Combinational, exploratory, and transformational."}, {"id": "w15d1", "week": 15, "day": 1, "t": "Why multimodal?", "l": "People combine sight, sound, and text; many decisions need the same. Multimodal AI joins data types, such as X-rays with clinical notes, or satellite images with weather readings, to predict better than any single source.", "r": [94], "s": "Read the Baltrušaitis et al. survey abstract and introduction.", "y": "Name one decision at work that uses more than one data type today.", "q": "What is the basic promise of multimodal AI?", "a": "Combining complementary data types gives better predictions than any one alone."}, {"id": "w15d2", "week": 15, "day": 2, "t": "The core challenges", "l": "The curriculum lists six challenges: representation (encoding each modality), translation (mapping between them), alignment (matching parts across them), fusion (combining them), co-learning (transferring knowledge between them), and reasoning across them.", "r": [95], "s": "Read the Liang, Zadeh and Morency abstract and the taxonomy figure.", "y": "Write the six challenges from memory, then check.", "q": "Which challenge is about combining modalities to make a prediction?", "a": "Fusion."}, {"id": "w15d3", "week": 15, "day": 3, "t": "Early, intermediate, and late fusion", "l": "Early fusion joins raw features before modeling. Intermediate fusion joins learned representations inside the model. Late fusion trains separate models and combines their predictions. Late fusion is simplest and robust when a modality is missing.", "r": [94], "s": "Read the fusion section of the Baltrušaitis et al. survey.", "y": "For tabular plus text data, sketch a late-fusion design with two models and an averaging step.", "q": "Which fusion type copes best with a missing modality?", "a": "Late fusion, since each model runs independently."}, {"id": "w15d4", "week": 15, "day": 4, "t": "HAIM in healthcare", "l": "The HAIM framework, co-authored by Bertsimas, combined tabular data, time series, text notes, and images from hospital records. Models using several modalities consistently beat single-modality models across many prediction tasks.", "r": [92], "s": "Read the HAIM paper abstract and its main results figure.", "y": "Write down which modality you think added the most in HAIM, then check the paper.", "q": "What was HAIM's headline finding?", "a": "Multimodal models outperformed single-modality models across the prediction tasks studied."}, {"id": "w15d5", "week": 15, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: modality ablation", "l": "An ablation removes one modality at a time and measures the drop in AUROC. It shows which data actually carries the signal and whether an expensive data source earns its keep.", "r": [93], "s": "Read the multimodal hurricane-forecasting paper abstract.", "y": "Phase 4 checkpoint: plan your multimodal notebook with modalities, fusion type, and the ablation table.", "q": "What does a modality ablation measure?", "a": "How much performance drops when a modality is removed."}, {"id": "w16d1", "week": 16, "day": 1, "t": "Compound AI systems", "l": "State-of-the-art results increasingly come from systems, not single models: an LLM plus retrieval, tools, and checks. Zaharia et al. call these compound AI systems. Designing the system is now as important as choosing the model.", "r": [101], "s": "Read The Shift from Models to Compound AI Systems.", "y": "Draw one AI product you use as boxes: model, data sources, tools, guardrails.", "q": "What is a compound AI system?", "a": "A system combining models with other components such as retrieval, tools, and verifiers."}, {"id": "w16d2", "week": 16, "day": 2, "t": "Retrieval-augmented generation", "l": "RAG retrieves relevant documents and gives them to the LLM with the question, so answers are grounded in your data and can cite sources. Quality depends heavily on how documents are chunked and retrieved.", "r": [104], "s": "Read the RAG paper abstract and Figure 1.", "y": "List three document sets at your organization that would make a useful RAG knowledge base.", "q": "What problem does RAG mainly address?", "a": "Answers not grounded in up-to-date or private knowledge, which leads to hallucinations."}, {"id": "w16d3", "week": 16, "day": 3, "t": "Hybrid search", "l": "Sparse retrieval (BM25) matches exact keywords and is good with names and codes. Dense retrieval matches meaning using embeddings. Hybrid search runs both and merges the rankings, often with Reciprocal Rank Fusion, which scores each document by 1/(k + rank) summed across lists.", "r": [106], "s": "Read the Reciprocal Rank Fusion paper (it is short).", "y": "Rank five documents two ways by hand and combine them with RRF using k = 60.", "q": "When does BM25 beat dense retrieval?", "a": "For exact terms such as product codes, names, and rare keywords."}, {"id": "w16d4", "week": 16, "day": 4, "t": "Agents and ReAct", "l": "ReAct interleaves reasoning and acting: the model writes a thought, takes an action (such as a search or tool call), reads the observation, and repeats. Anthropic recommends starting with simple, composable workflows and adding autonomy only when needed.", "r": [107, 102], "s": "Read Anthropic's Building Effective Agents.", "y": "Write a three-step ReAct trace for 'What is our refund policy for damaged goods?'", "q": "Name the three parts of the ReAct loop.", "a": "Thought, action, and observation."}, {"id": "w16d5", "week": 16, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: RAG topologies", "l": "Standard RAG retrieves chunks. Contextual retrieval adds document context to each chunk before indexing. GraphRAG builds a knowledge graph for global questions. Agentic RAG lets an agent decide what to retrieve and when.", "r": [103, 109], "s": "Read Anthropic's Contextual Retrieval post.", "y": "Draft an evaluation set: ten questions with expected answers for your RAG demo.", "q": "What does contextual retrieval add to each chunk?", "a": "A short description of where the chunk sits in its source document."}, {"id": "w17d1", "week": 17, "day": 1, "t": "Interpretable or explained?", "l": "Intrinsically interpretable models (short trees, sparse linear models) are understandable by design. Post-hoc methods explain black boxes after the fact. Rudin argues that for high-stakes decisions you should prefer interpretable models where they perform comparably.", "r": [121], "s": "Read Rudin's Stop Explaining Black Box Models, the abstract and introduction.", "y": "Name one high-stakes decision where you would require an interpretable model.", "q": "What is Rudin's core recommendation?", "a": "Use inherently interpretable models for high-stakes decisions instead of explaining black boxes."}, {"id": "w17d2", "week": 17, "day": 2, "t": "SHAP and LIME", "l": "LIME explains one prediction by fitting a simple model around it. SHAP assigns each feature a contribution based on Shapley values from game theory, so contributions add up to the prediction. Both give local explanations; SHAP values can also be aggregated globally.", "r": [118, 117], "s": "Read Molnar's Interpretable Machine Learning, the SHAP chapter introduction.", "y": "Run SHAP on a small tree model and read the top three features for one prediction.", "q": "What do SHAP values for one prediction add up to?", "a": "The difference between that prediction and the average prediction."}, {"id": "w17d3", "week": 17, "day": 3, "t": "Counterfactual explanations", "l": "A counterfactual explanation says what would need to change for a different outcome, for example 'with income of 5,000 more, the loan would be approved'. Wachter et al. argue these help people understand and contest decisions without opening the model.", "r": [120], "s": "Read the Wachter et al. abstract.", "y": "Write a counterfactual explanation for a declined insurance application.", "q": "What does a counterfactual explanation tell a person?", "a": "The smallest change to their inputs that would change the outcome."}, {"id": "w17d4", "week": 17, "day": 4, "t": "Fairness metrics", "l": "Demographic parity asks for equal positive rates across groups. Equalized odds asks for equal true-positive and false-positive rates. Disparate impact compares selection rates; the four-fifths rule flags a ratio below 0.8.", "r": [116, 123], "s": "Read Fairness and Machine Learning, the classification chapter introduction.", "y": "Compute disparate impact for a group selected at 30% versus 45%. Does it pass the four-fifths rule?", "q": "Under the four-fifths rule, what ratio triggers concern?", "a": "A selection-rate ratio below 0.8 (80%). In the try-it, 30/45 is about 0.67, so it fails."}, {"id": "w17d5", "week": 17, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: the COMPAS case", "l": "ProPublica found the COMPAS risk tool had higher false-positive rates for Black defendants. The vendor showed scores were equally calibrated across groups. Both were true, which sets up next week's impossibility results.", "r": [125], "s": "Read ProPublica's Machine Bias article.", "y": "Outline your fairness audit report: dataset, groups, three metrics, findings.", "q": "Why could both sides of the COMPAS debate be right?", "a": "They used different fairness criteria (error-rate balance versus calibration), which can conflict."}, {"id": "w18d1", "week": 18, "day": 1, "t": "Three ethical lenses", "l": "Deontology judges actions by duties and rules. Utilitarianism judges by outcomes, aiming for the greatest overall good. Virtue ethics asks what a person of good character would do. Each highlights different risks in AI decisions.", "r": [139], "s": "Read the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry Ethics of AI and Robotics, Section 1.", "y": "Analyze one AI use case through all three lenses in one sentence each.", "q": "Which lens focuses on consequences?", "a": "Utilitarianism."}, {"id": "w18d2", "week": 18, "day": 2, "t": "Where bias comes from", "l": "Suresh and Guttag map harm across the machine learning lifecycle. The curriculum stresses four sources: historical bias (the world itself), sampling or representation bias (who is in the data), measurement bias (flawed proxies), and aggregation bias (one model for different groups). Their paper describes seven in total.", "r": [127], "s": "Read the Suresh and Guttag framework figure and its descriptions.", "y": "Pick a dataset you know and name the most likely source of bias in it.", "q": "What is measurement bias?", "a": "Bias from using a flawed proxy for the thing you actually care about."}, {"id": "w18d3", "week": 18, "day": 3, "t": "The impossibility results", "l": "Chouldechova and Kleinberg et al. showed that when groups have different base rates, a risk score cannot satisfy calibration and equal error rates at the same time, except in trivial cases. Fairness requires choosing which criterion matters for the context.", "r": [128, 129], "s": "Read the Kleinberg, Mullainathan and Raghavan abstract.", "y": "Write which criterion you would prioritize for a hiring screen, and why.", "q": "What condition triggers the impossibility?", "a": "Different base rates between groups (outside trivial cases such as a perfect predictor)."}, {"id": "w18d4", "week": 18, "day": 4, "t": "Constitutional AI", "l": "Constitutional AI trains a model to critique and revise its own outputs against a written set of principles, reducing reliance on human labels for harmlessness. It makes the values being optimized explicit and reviewable.", "r": [136], "s": "Read the Constitutional AI abstract and Figure 1.", "y": "Write three principles you would put in a constitution for an internal AI assistant.", "q": "What replaces much of the human feedback in Constitutional AI?", "a": "AI feedback guided by a written set of principles."}, {"id": "w18d5", "week": 18, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: aggregating values", "l": "Arrow's theorem shows no voting rule can satisfy a small set of reasonable fairness conditions at once when there are three or more options. Conitzer et al. argue social choice theory should guide how AI aligns to conflicting human preferences.", "r": [140, 138], "s": "Read the Stanford Encyclopedia entry on Arrow's Theorem, the introduction.", "y": "Outline your 2-page ethics position paper: claim, framework, counterargument, recommendation.", "q": "Why does Arrow's theorem matter for AI alignment?", "a": "It shows there is no perfect way to aggregate conflicting human preferences, so trade-offs must be chosen deliberately."}, {"id": "w19d1", "week": 19, "day": 1, "t": "NIST AI RMF: Govern", "l": "The NIST AI Risk Management Framework has four functions: Govern, Map, Measure, and Manage. Govern is the foundation: policies, roles, accountability, and culture for AI risk across the organization.", "r": [142], "s": "Read the NIST AI RMF overview page and the Govern function summary.", "y": "List who owns AI risk decisions in your organization today, or note the gap.", "q": "Name the four NIST AI RMF functions.", "a": "Govern, Map, Measure, and Manage."}, {"id": "w19d2", "week": 19, "day": 2, "t": "Map, Measure, Manage", "l": "Map establishes context and identifies risks for each use. Measure assesses and tracks them, which is where your fairness and explainability work fits. Manage prioritizes and acts on the risks, including decisions not to deploy.", "r": [142], "s": "Read the NIST AI RMF Core summaries for Map, Measure, and Manage.", "y": "Map your Week 17 fairness audit to the Measure function in two sentences.", "q": "Which function covers testing for bias and performance?", "a": "Measure."}, {"id": "w19d3", "week": 19, "day": 3, "t": "The EU AI Act's risk tiers", "l": "The EU AI Act sorts AI systems by risk: prohibited practices, high-risk systems with strict obligations, limited-risk systems with transparency duties, and minimal-risk systems. Credit scoring and some insurance pricing uses fall in the high-risk list.", "r": [143], "s": "Use the EU AI Act explorer to read the high-risk annex list.", "y": "Classify three AI use cases at your organization into the Act's tiers.", "q": "What are the EU AI Act's main risk categories?", "a": "Unacceptable (prohibited), high, limited (transparency), and minimal risk."}, {"id": "w19d4", "week": 19, "day": 4, "t": "ISO/IEC 42001", "l": "ISO/IEC 42001 is a management-system standard for AI, structured like ISO 27001. It requires an AI policy, risk and impact assessments, defined roles, controls, monitoring, and continual improvement, and organizations can be certified against it.", "r": [144], "s": "Read the ISO/IEC 42001 overview page.", "y": "List five controls you would put first in an AI management system.", "q": "What kind of standard is ISO/IEC 42001?", "a": "A certifiable management-system standard for AI."}, {"id": "w19d5", "week": 19, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: risk-tiering rubric", "l": "A risk-tiering rubric scores each AI use case on factors such as impact on people, autonomy, data sensitivity, and reversibility, and assigns controls by tier. It turns principles into a repeatable intake process.", "r": [142], "s": "No new reading. Use this session to draft.", "y": "Phase 5 checkpoint: draft your governance playbook rubric with four factors, three tiers, and controls per tier.", "q": "Name two factors a risk-tiering rubric should score.", "a": "For example: impact on people and degree of autonomy (also data sensitivity, reversibility)."}, {"id": "w20d1", "week": 20, "day": 1, "t": "Choose your capstone", "l": "The capstone combines predictive, prescriptive, generative, and governance work on one real problem. Pick a domain you know well, such as insurance, health, or finance, so your judgment adds value.", "r": [7], "s": "Review the elective list on the program page to pick a domain.", "y": "Write the capstone problem in one sentence with the decision it improves.", "q": "Which four strands should the capstone integrate?", "a": "Predictive, prescriptive, generative, and governance work."}, {"id": "w20d2", "week": 20, "day": 2, "t": "Elective deep dive", "l": "Pick one free elective course: AI and finance, machine learning for healthcare, or new enterprises. Use it to borrow domain cases and vocabulary for your capstone.", "r": [145, 146, 147], "s": "Watch the first lecture of your chosen elective course.", "y": "Note two ideas from the lecture that apply to your capstone.", "q": "Why take an elective before finishing the capstone?", "a": "It supplies domain cases and vocabulary that make the solution credible."}, {"id": "w20d3", "week": 20, "day": 3, "t": "Build the solution", "l": "Keep scope small: one model, one decision, one generative feature, and the controls around them. A finished small system beats an unfinished large one.", "r": [19], "s": "No new reading. Build session.", "y": "Get the end-to-end path working once, even with rough parts.", "q": "What should you prioritize first in the build?", "a": "A working end-to-end path, before polishing any single part."}, {"id": "w20d4", "week": 20, "day": 4, "t": "Model card and risk assessment", "l": "Document the model (purpose, data, metrics, limits) and assess risk using your Week 19 rubric. Name who is accountable and how the system will be monitored.", "r": [142], "s": "Review your Week 4 model card and Week 19 rubric.", "y": "Complete the model card and risk assessment for the capstone.", "q": "What two documents accompany the capstone solution?", "a": "A model card and a risk assessment."}, {"id": "w20d5", "week": 20, "day": 5, "t": "Recap: the executive brief", "l": "Close with a one-page executive brief: the decision, the value, the risks and controls, and the ask. You have now covered the full arc from Python to governance.", "r": [1], "s": "No new reading. Writing session.", "y": "Write the brief, then explain your capstone aloud in two minutes without notes.", "q": "What four elements belong in the executive brief?", "a": "The decision, the value, the risks and controls, and the ask."}], "science": [{"id": "ls1", "author": "Dunlosky, Rawson, Marsh, Nathan & Willingham", "title": "Improving Students’ Learning With Effective Learning Techniques", "suffix": "(Psychological Science in the Public Interest, 2013)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/1529100612453266"}, {"id": "ls2", "author": "Cepeda, Pashler, Vul, Wixted & Rohrer", "title": "Distributed Practice in Verbal Recall Tasks: A Review and Quantitative Synthesis", "suffix": "(Psychological Bulletin, 2006)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.132.3.354"}, {"id": "ls3", "author": "Roediger & Karpicke", "title": "Test-Enhanced Learning", "suffix": "(Psychological Science, 2006)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9280.2006.01693.x"}]};
